{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 119 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6bf55fb830653b5a0f86128cafc8d3671805502c64b8a8db6cf3d54563788ebf" :edb/db-before-t 118 :edb/db-after-t 119 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533431 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:46:44.854Z" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186054760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354519 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186054823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354479 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186054823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354480 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186054824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354478 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186059770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354527 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186059807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354296 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186059850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354471 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186059851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354470 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186059851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354472 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186060430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059172 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186060430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354245 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186060430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186060430 1071 "Given that $\\frac{13\\textrm{i}}{2 - 3\\textrm{i}} = p + \\textrm{i}q$, calculate $p$ and $q$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186060430 1071 "Given that $\\frac{13\\text{i}}{2-3\\text{i}}=p+\\text{i}q,$ calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186060430 1074 "First, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13\\text{i}(2+3\\text{i})}{(2-3\\text{i})(2+3\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{13\\text{i}(2+3\\text{i})}{(2)^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{13\\text{i}(2+3\\text{i})}{13} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\text{i}(2+3\\text{i}) & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 2\\text{i}+3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ -3+2\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $-3=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $2=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3$ and $q=2.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354301 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186064293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354435 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186064293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354436 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354466 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354467 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186068810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354459 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186068867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354474 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078490 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354421 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078491 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354422 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078495 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354469 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078495 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354481 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078522 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354549 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354461 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354462 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354463 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078605 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354464 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186078605 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354465 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186079219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186078062 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186079219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354175 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186079219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186079219 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186079219 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 3x - 4y, y(1) = 2$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | $$? |" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186079219 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354420 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354429 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354526 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354537 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354383 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354384 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354385 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354386 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354387 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354388 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354393 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354374 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354375 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354376 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354377 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354522 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354523 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354328 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354329 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354570 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354571 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354572 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354285 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354288 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354291 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354392 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354389 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354390 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354391 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082607 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353855 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083951 1071 "After entering a competition, Mick wins an all-inclusive vacation for himself and four friends. He creates a list of seven people that he would like to take with him. From how many different groups of four friends does Mick have to choose?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083951 1071 "After entering a competition, Mick wins an all-inclusive vacation for himself and four friends. He creates a list of seven people that he would like to take with him. From how many different groups of four friends does Mick have to choose?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083951 1074 "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ friends, so $n=7.$ We want to select $4$ of those friends for each group, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different groups of four friends is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{3!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{3!\\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{6}\\cdot 5}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082619 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354085 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083953 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y = \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + e^{-x})$ for $x ∈ [0, \\ln 2]$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083953 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083953 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})-({e}^{0}-{e}^{-0})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082655 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354052 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083959 1071 "In a class, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them play soccer, and of the girls, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them play soccer. What is the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083959 1071 "In a class, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them play soccer, and of the girls, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them play soccer. What is the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083959 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen student plays soccer, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the boys play soccer and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the girls play soccer, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|B)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(S|{B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082667 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354250 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083961 1071 "In a class, $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{12}$ of them play basketball. The probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15}$. If a girl is selected at random, what is the probability that she plays basketball?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186083961 1071 "In a class, $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{12}$ of them play basketball. The probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15}.$ If a girl is selected at random, what is the probability that she plays basketball?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186083961 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that the chosen student plays basketball, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{12}$ of the boys play basketball, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(A|{B}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =(\\frac{1}{12})(\\frac{3}{5})+P(A|{B}^{′})(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ 60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{15}) & =60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{′}))​ \\\\ 4 & =3+24P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ 1 & =24P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ P(A|{B}^{′}) & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected girl plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{24}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354617 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354623 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354624 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354490 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354488 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354489 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354276 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354432 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354433 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354434 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354295 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354320 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354321 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354322 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354325 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354326 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354327 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354331 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354332 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354333 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354334 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354286 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354287 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354289 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354290 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354292 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354293 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354577 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186087893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354650 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087209 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354121 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088431 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $((x - 2)^{2})/(5) - ((y + 4)^{2})/(16) = 1$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088431 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16}=1.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088431 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, there are no $y$-intercepts." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087215 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354113 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088432 1071 "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $14x^{2} - 3y^{2} = 50$ intersects the line $y = 2x$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088432 1071 "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $14{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=50$ intersects the line $y=2x.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088432 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=2x,$ we first replace $y$ with $2x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =50 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-3(2x)^{2} & =50 \\\\ 14{x}^{2}-12{x}^{2} & =50 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =50 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087227 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354091 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088434 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{y^{2}}{18} - \\frac{x^{2}}{81} = 1$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088434 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}=1.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088434 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2},\\,b=\\sqrt{81}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{9}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087233 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353828 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088435 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088435 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x =-\\frac{1}{4}t, y =-\\frac{1}{8}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088435 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-4x,\\,{t}^{2}=-8y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =16{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-8y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2} & =-8y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087239 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354128 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088436 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0, 0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)$?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088436 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088436 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087251 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354184 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088438 1071 "![](images/8e/8e85666a72dde1ebbe24ff3d8dbb1404f010e81d9406aec790be11416b705f00.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088438 1071 "![](images/8e/8e85666a72dde1ebbe24ff3d8dbb1404f010e81d9406aec790be11416b705f00.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088438 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(3,-6),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -6=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087263 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353957 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088440 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z = 7 - 5\\textrm{i}$.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0, 2π)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088440 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=7-5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088440 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d096d76118bc06b2d5c79d5017c0178d96fc84bff6f97d563b7b872d26e75d20.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{7})≈0.620\\,25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.620\\,25≈5.66\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087269 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354261 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088441 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088441 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\frac{1}{4}t^{2}, y = \\frac{1}{2}t, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088441 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4x,\\,t=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=x.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087287 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354240 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088444 1074 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=x.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $1.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =tx=tf(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087293 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354181 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088445 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088445 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sec (\\frac{x}{y}) + \\frac{y}{x}$.\n\nIf the transformation $y = v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088445 1074 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{vx})+\\frac{vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087329 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353903 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088451 1071 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186088451 1071 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y^{2} - x^{2}}{xy}$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186088451 1074 "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}-1)}{v{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1}{v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1-{v}^{2}}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354307 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354313 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354439 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354441 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354311 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354525 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354540 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354555 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354560 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354562 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354423 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354424 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354431 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354437 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354438 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092200 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354367 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354493 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186092223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354410 1319413