2186157437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354614 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354615 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354616 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354618 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354612 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354619 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354620 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354651 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354652 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354653 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354654 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354601 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354621 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354622 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354574 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354606 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354607 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354608 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354609 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354591 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354593 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354594 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354595 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354597 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354598 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155985 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354124 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157849 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y - 1)^{2} =-5(x - 1)$?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157849 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-1)^{2}=-5(x-1)?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157849 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p(x-1) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p & =-5 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(-\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-\\frac{5}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155997 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354235 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157851 1071 "![](images/75/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157851 1071 "![](images/75/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157851 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(5,-1),$ and passes through the point $(17,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(5,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(17,11),$ we can substitute $x=17$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156003 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353993 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157852 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-6=3t,\\,y-3=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157852 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x - 6 = 3t, y - 3 = \\frac{3}{2}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157852 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-6}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-6}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y-3)}{3} \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}) \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =6(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156009 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353945 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157853 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=3t,\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157853 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x - 2 = 3t, y + 2 = \\frac{3}{2}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157853 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-2}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y+2)}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156123 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353991 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157872 1071 "At a water bottle factory, every batch of $50$ water bottles has $4$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle they check? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157872 1071 "At a water bottle factory, every batch of $50$ water bottles has $4$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle they check? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157872 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bottles checked until a defect is found. On average, the chance of finding an upside down label on any water bottle is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{50}=0.08.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of bottles that the inspector checks before finding an upside down label can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.08.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.08).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.08)^{x-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{x-1}(0.08)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first upside down label on the $5$th bottle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{5-1}(0.08) \\\\  & =(0.92)^{4}(0.08) \\\\  & ≈0.057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156129 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354102 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157873 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $15%$ chance of having a typo. What is the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences since the previous typo? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157873 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $15%$ chance of having a typo. What is the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences since the previous typo? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157873 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $15%,$ the number of sentences since the previous typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=15%=\\frac{15}{100}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156141 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353860 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157875 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157875 1071 "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, each sentence has a $5%$ chance of containing a typo. What is the probability of finding the first typo in the $4$th sentence or later? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157875 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $5%,$ the number of sentences checked until the first typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=5%=\\frac{5}{100}=0.05.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.05).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.05)^{x-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{x-1}(0.05)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require $P(X\\ge 4).$ \n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.95)^{1-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{2-1}(0.05)+(0.95)^{3-1}(0.05) \\\\  & =(0.05)+(0.95)(0.05)+(0.95)^{2}(0.05) \\\\  & =[1+0.95+(0.95)^{2}](0.05) \\\\  & ≈0.143,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, there are no typos within the first three sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.143 \\\\  & ≈0.857\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156255 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354032 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157894 1071 "Solve the inequality $(5 - 2x)^{3} - 60 \\ge 4$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157894 1071 "Solve the inequality $(5-2x)^{3}-60\\ge 4.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157894 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-2x)^{3}-60 & \\ge 4 \\\\ (5-2x)^{3} & \\ge 64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the cube root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(5-2x)^{3}} & \\ge \\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ 5-2x & \\ge 4 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156285 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354193 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157899 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x + 1} + 1 \\ge - 5$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157899 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1\\ge -5.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157899 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1 & \\ge -5 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3x+1} & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+1})}^{2} & \\le {3}^{2} \\\\ 3x+1 & \\le 9 \\\\ 3x & \\le 8 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{8}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}]\\cap [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )=[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156363 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354203 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157912 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\ln (x + 2) - 5 \\ge \\ln (3x)$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157912 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157912 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}\\cdot {e}^{-5} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} \\\\ (x+2){e}^{-5} & \\ge 3x \\\\ x+2 & \\ge 3{e}^{5}x \\\\ 2 & \\ge (3{e}^{5}-1)x \\\\ \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1} & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-2 \\\\ 3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-2$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\\cap (-2,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=(0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (0,\\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}],$ which can also be written as $0<x\\le \\frac{2}{3{e}^{5}-1}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156645 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353865 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157959 1071 "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-7}^{7}3x^{6}dx$ using symmetry." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157959 1071 "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-7}^{7}3{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157959 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3{x}^{6}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{6} \\\\  & =3{x}^{6} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3{x}^{6}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}3{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{7}3{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156651 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354040 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157960 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157960 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}3x^{2}dx$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^{6}5x^{3}dx$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-9}^{9}2x^{5}dx$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157960 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156705 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354087 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157969 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186157969 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}\\sin^{2} xdx$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^{3}\\tan (2x)dx$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{4}4\\tan xdx$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186157969 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186171965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354483 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186171966 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354658 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186171966 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354659 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186172026 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354401 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354303 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175958 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354304 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175958 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354305 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354308 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175959 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354309 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354299 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354283 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186175965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354541 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354268 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354269 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354270 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354456 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354266 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354267 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354298 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186179683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354455 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186184181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354521 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186184182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354528 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354475 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186184213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354524 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186192760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354561 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186192761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354564 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186192761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354565 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354520 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192267 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354238 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193395 1071 "Compute $P(2 < X < 8)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{1}{50}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193395 1071 "Compute $P(2<X<8)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{50}x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193395 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<8) & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{1}{50}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{100}{x}^{2}]}_{2}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192273 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354108 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193396 1071 "Compute $P(X \\ge 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = {8e^{-8x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x < 0$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193396 1071 "Compute $P(X\\ge 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-8x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193396 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(5\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-8x}]}_{5}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192279 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354163 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193397 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193397 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n$f(x) = {ke^{-3x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193397 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-3x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186192285 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354046 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193398 1071 "Compute $P(X > 8)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{3}{x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x < 3$." 13194139533431 17592186045585 false] [17592186193398 1071 "Compute $P(X>8)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186193398 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(8<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{x}]}_{8}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186198453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354567 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186234473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354097 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186351566 1081 "\\sqrt{6}+5" 13194139533431 1759218604558