igned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354070 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354359 1069 "q-84844" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354359 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-3)^{2}=-12(x-5)?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354359 1072 #uuid "1303c715-2341-5c20-852f-40641cdc1a09" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354359 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,3).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p(x-5) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p & =-12 \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353888 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354360 1069 "q-84847" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354360 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-2)^{2}=16(x+6)?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354360 1072 #uuid "f197f0f5-2e24-5c55-8528-25906de0f7eb" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354360 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+6=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p(x+6) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p & =16 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+6=(4){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(4)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353940 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354361 1069 "q-84851" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354361 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354361 1072 #uuid "bd8e3dfd-6f7e-504a-b98a-c0593b85e82f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354361 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4} & =y-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353917 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354362 1069 "q-84853" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354362 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2{t}^{2},\\,y-4=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354362 1072 #uuid "e3011f06-5149-58bf-a601-8ad1e445dd9a" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354362 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-4}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+3}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+3}{2} \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+3}{2}) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =8(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353819 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354363 1069 "q-84854" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354363 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+5=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354363 1072 #uuid "2f6ea62c-3d64-50ec-b882-0c7611fa1214" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354363 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+5}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(y+2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4} & =-(y+2) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =-4(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354024 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354364 1069 "q-84855" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354364 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-3=-6t,\\,y-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354364 1072 #uuid "39c29706-500e-5209-8aff-2370c6bcdd81" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354364 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-3}{6},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-3}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =-\\frac{y-1}{3} \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{y-1}{3}) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354182 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354365 1069 "q-84858" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354365 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354365 1072 #uuid "498096d3-80ed-51eb-b10a-90d7f551479a" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354365 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y-1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x-4}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-4}{2}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-8(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353824 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354366 1069 "q-84859" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354366 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354366 1072 #uuid "5f7f6549-2721-5283-9cbb-bd6bbc1d1262" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354366 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the 