a is the right-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/e4/e4018ea79601a725ad4586eda123eaa5900a171bbce505c4c58c3bf80af7e6a8.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-1,-3).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354030 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354371 1069 "q-84952" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354371 1071 "![](images/83/83217df9245edba7b09f5b29edd8862564c3c4e772ba2da45e1c4e11f10c7747.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354371 1072 #uuid "4c3b2857-f742-54ff-a16a-524aeaa787ac" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354371 1074 "The vertex of a left-opening parabola is the right-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/80/80c3336af95cf45198f4d8cf3b8718c7dcd2512f4720948d0edda3d987a6aa0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(0,5).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354207 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354372 1069 "q-84953" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354372 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=-2y?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354372 1072 #uuid "3834474f-27e5-5548-9ec5-2158ba9e8e69" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354372 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(-\\frac{1}{2})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354216 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354373 1069 "q-84954" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354373 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=5y?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354373 1072 #uuid "4a3d9a2c-1761-5baf-9e5d-4958b26130d5" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354373 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354263 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354374 1069 "q-84956" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354374 1071 "A pizzeria has nine different toppings from which to choose. How many different pizzas can be made using only three toppings?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354374 1072 #uuid "a9b3fff3-7ca8-5a05-bacf-2186c0ce6253" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354374 1074 "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ toppings, so $n=9.$ We want to select $3$ of those toppings for each of the pizzas, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different pizzas is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{6!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{504}{6} \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354143 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354375 1069 "q-84959" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354375 1071 "Sara has a box of $9$ colored pencils. In how many ways can Sara pick a set of $4$ different pencils from the box?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354375 1072 #uuid "44e1ceb1-ad30-5708-98e7-b7ac9be19ae7" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354375 1074 "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ pencils in the box, so $n=9.$ We want to select $4$ of those pencils, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of four pencils is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,024}{24} \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354187 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354376 1069 "q-84961" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354376 1071 "In a high school, eight players enter trials for the soccer team. In how many ways can the coach select four players to add to the team?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354376 1072 #uuid "40656786-fca3-5024-b3bd-e2f484a07204" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354376 1074 "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $8$ players, so $n=8.$ We want to select $4$ of those players for the team, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different selections of four players is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{8}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{8!}{(8-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8!}{4!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{4!\\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,680}{24} \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353872 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354377 1069 "q-84964" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354377 1071 "Given any two points in a plane, there is a unique line that passes through both points. Given six points in a plane, what is the maximum number of unique  lines that can be made using these points?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354377 1072 #uuid "a3962390-93bb-5eee-a524-e14f0426b20f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354377 1074 "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ points, so $n=6.$ We want to select $2$ of those points for each of the lines, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of unique lines is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{4!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354225 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354378 1069 "q-84966" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354378 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}(y-1)?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354378 1072 #uuid "a9c37a31-cbf7-579c-82f4-1ccdb89a382e" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354378 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{8})(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=0$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(0,1).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353946 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354379 1069 "q-84967" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354379 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+3)^{2}=5y?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354379 1072 #uuid "f2eb1bd6-8295-533c-aa8b-65d0d7da5480" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354379 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})(y-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-3$ and $k=0.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-3,0).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354144 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354380 1069 "q-84969" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354380 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x-1)^{2}=2(y-4)?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354380 1072 #uuid "86b88f9c-a48e-50fc-bffe-5ae45a172150" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354380 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=1$ and $k=4.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(1,4).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354083 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354381 1069 "q-84975" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354381 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-8x?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354381 1072 #uuid "8a083623-223f-5e1c-9de8-75212036d214" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354381 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(-2)x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-2.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354255 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354382 1069 "q-84977" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354382 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-4)^{2}=-3(x+1)?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354382 1072 #uuid "284a7e70-dfc0-5ece-990d-e051558b1b03" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354382 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4(-\\frac{3}{4})(x+1).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-1$ and $k=4.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-1,4).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354239 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354383 1069 "q-84978" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354383 1071 "Diana is choosing a $4$-digit passcode for her cellphone from the digits ${1,2,3,4,5,6},$ where no digit is used more than once. How many different passcodes are possible?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354383 1072 #uuid "147e6481-6004-5101-9123-dba8267b88a0" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354383 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ digits, so $n=6.$ We want to select $4$ of those digits for the passcode, so we have $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different passcodes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =360.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353820 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354384 1069 "q-84979" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354384 1071 "Of the $7$ students who are going to take a test, the top $2$ will get different prizes. In how many ways can both prizes be awarded?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354384 1072 #uuid "2cf8e6b1-9c83-5917-8b55-e1f57bf1150c" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354384 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ students, so $n=7.$ We want to select $2$ of those students for both the prizes, so we have $r=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to award the prizes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{P}_{2} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353837 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354385 1069 "q-84980" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354385 1071 "How many made-up $3$-letter words can be made using only $5$ vowels, where each vowel cannot appear in the word more than once?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354385 1072 #uuid "fda58513-e80c-5984-860c-d5cee3ac12ce" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354385 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $5$ vowels, so $n=5.$ We want to select $3$ of those vowels to make-up a $3$-letter word, so we have $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different made-up words is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353871 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354386 1069 "q-84982" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354386 1071 "Victoria has the $4$ playing cards: the ace of clubs, the ace of diamonds, the ace of hearts, and the ace of spades. She takes one card and places it in front of her. She then does the same with another card. In how many different ways can she choose a sequence of $2$ cards like this?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354386 1072 #uuid "29047b99-0717-54ea-8401-af32e706bd20" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354386 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $4$ cards, so $n=4.$ We want to select $2$ of those cards to make a sequence of $2$ cards, so we have $r=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to choose the cards is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{4}{P}_{2} & =\\frac{4!}{(4-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353835 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354387 1069 "q-84987" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354387 1071 "How many made-up $4$-letter words can be made using $21$ consonants, where each consonant cannot appear in the word more than once?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354387 1072 #uuid "78722b60-c5d9-5236-8510-29988783d34e" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354387 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $21$ consonants, so $n=21.$ We want to select $4$ of those consonants to make-up a $4$-letter word, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different made-up words is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{21}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{21!}{(21-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{21!}{17!} \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\cdot 20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot \\cancel{17!}}{\\cancel{17!}} \\\\  & =21\\cdot 20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =143\\,640.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354096 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354388 1069 "q-84989" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354388 1071 "The $12$ members of a student council are asked to elect among themselves a leadership team: a president, a vice-president, and a secretary. How many different leadership teams are possible?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354388 1072 #uuid "99b2f516-70c2-5e55-a428-0efe2c5f0628" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354388 1074 "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ members to choose from, so $n=12.$ We want to select $3$ of those members to be in the leadership team, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different $3$-member leadership teams is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =1\\,320.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354161 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354389 1069 "q-84993" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354389 1071 "Evaluate $(\\frac{25}{0}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354389 1072 #uuid "e1b1d463-34ec-526e-825f-fea323344b88" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354389 1074 "First, note that $(\\frac{25}{0})$ means the same thing as ${}_{25}{C}_{0}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=25$ and $r=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{25}{C}_{0} & =\\frac{25!}{(25-0)!0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{25!}{25!0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{25!}}{\\cancel{25!}\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353936 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354390 1069 "q-84994" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354390 1071 "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354390 1072 #uuid "57957360-6f31-5e05-8a45-38b0c037b34e" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354390 1073 true 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354390 1074 "First, note that $(\\frac{12}{3})$ means the same thing as ${}_{12}{C}_{3}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $r=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10}{6} \\\\  & =220.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353879 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354391 1069 "q-85041" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354391 1071 "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354391 1072 #uuid "14b01419-a43a-5786-a508-3298f924302b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354391 1074 "First, note that $(\\frac{36}{33})$ means the same thing as ${}_{36}{C}_{33}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=36$ and $r=33,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{36}{C}_{33} & =\\frac{36!}{(36-33)!33!} \\\\  & =\\frac{36!}{3!33!} \\\\  & =\\frac{36\\cdot 35\\cdot 34\\cdot \\cancel{33!}}{3!\\cdot \\cancel{33!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36\\cdot 35\\cdot 34}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{42\\,840}{6} \\\\  & =7\\,140.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354149 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354392 1069 "q-85045" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354392 1071 "${}_{40}{P}_{3}=$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354392 1072 #uuid "6e4aeb4d-e285-59ac-8418-fed62fe6019b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354392 1074 "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=40$ and $r=3$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{40}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{40!}{(40-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{40!}{37!} \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\cdot 39\\cdot 38\\cdot \\cancel{37!}}{\\cancel{37!}} \\\\  & =40\\cdot 39\\cdot 38 \\\\  & =59\\,280.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354208 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354393 1069 "q-85048" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354393 1071 "$P(45,0)=$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354393 1072 #uuid "44aa0f86-d6a0-5e26-9c99-e34cfef15011" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354393 1074 "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\n$P(45,0)$ means the same thing as ${}_{45}{P}_{0}.$ Using the usual formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(45,0) & =\\frac{45!}{(45-0)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{45!}}{\\cancel{45!}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354056 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354394 1069 "q-8506" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354394 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-8,-15).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to two decimal places where necessary." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354394 1072 #uuid "4a198cb5-76b0-5dae-afeb-ae6dcb19298a" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354394 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(-15)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+225} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{289} \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-15}{-8})| \\\\  & ≈1.080\\,84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the third quadrant, we add the reference angle to $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.080\\,84 \\\\  & ≈4.22,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(17,4.22).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354249 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354395 1069 "q-85060" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354395 1071 "![](images/0d/0d91ac9127718b0f2755ec14c268d23af810c2ec6f13b4935930b4b348b76b34.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354395 1072 #uuid "7722548e-2e3e-577a-9b3d-e7c3bfd357b6" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354395 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353915 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354396 1069 "q-85065" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354396 1071 "![](images/15/15e58073c87bd249473950680a49e8fc4cb4be94277a7001c0ab9f96ab3a46df.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354396 1072 #uuid "c5f310f9-e63f-56fe-abce-21ad9b7c4099" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354396 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-3,-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/92/928c6dc79f44a2185ccf217c0cf9210630b6554a698f805ef2c77500f9ddc76b.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354135 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354397 1069 "q-85067" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354397 1071 "![](images/dc/dc35d0362ef95d11402ad44cded01abf46686d3088e9d852bd0b9a7ba2454e16.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354397 1072 #uuid "5d05c396-81e6-5aa1-b630-71a86aa552fd" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354397 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/ca/ca09f228fc218ef1b57196c6b6b1e740c10173b7cd38b1c27407726eccd783f1.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353921 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354398 1069 "q-85069" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354398 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354398 1072 #uuid "f6f8c2df-477d-5e6f-b732-724c19c5001f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354398 1074 "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|8\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-2)| \\\\  & =|24+10| \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353847 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354399 1069 "q-85073" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354399 1071 "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(-2,-1),(3,-1),(1,3).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354399 1072 #uuid "f868f537-2010-5643-8674-600ac4cecd5d" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354399 1074 "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/10/10c831c616279f443d10ed478a8b3b173d598da6e9b0b45edb7ecf487cf97f92.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4=10.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,6\\,|\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354009 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354400 1069 "q-85077" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354400 1071 "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354400 1072 #uuid "72560fde-0974-5374-a6a6-4ed2719dd357" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354400 1074 "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353934 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354401 1069 "q-85080" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354401 1071 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $5{x}^{2}+30x+2y+41=0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354401 1072 #uuid "2b03beb9-8f72-5245-9a15-2a9bcfedfa5d" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354401 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+30x+2y+41 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+30x & =-2y-41 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}+6x] & =-2y-41 \\\\ 5[(x+3)^{2}-9] & =-2y-41 \\\\ 5(x+3)^{2}-45 & =-2y-41 \\\\ 5(x+3)^{2} & =-2y-41+45 \\\\ 5(x+3)^{2} & =-2y+4 \\\\ 5(x+3)^{2} & =-2(y-2) \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{5}(y-2) \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{10})(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-3,2).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354080 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354402 1069 "q-85082" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354402 1071 "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354402 1072 #uuid "e18e3b37-f258-59be-baac-0eea1b123ca9" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354402 1074 "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{18-16}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353972 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354403 1069 "q-8511" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354403 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(4,-1).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to two decimal places where necessary." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354403 1072 #uuid "31ef0ce9-3cb7-5d40-b6f9-27a019068ea7" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354403 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17} \\\\  & =4.123\\,10… \\\\  & ≈4.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{4})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-0.25)| \\\\  & =|-0.244\\,97…| \\\\  & ≈0.244\\,97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point lies in the fourth quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.244\\,97 \\\\  & ≈6.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(4.12,6.04),$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353952 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354404 1069 "q-8516" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354404 1071 "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${150}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354404 1072 #uuid "2ab581aa-df37-556d-9193-036f42c477b8" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354404 1074 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta ={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354028 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354405 1069 "q-85170" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354405 1071 "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354405 1072 #uuid "5b9b4918-d355-5d49-9319-d404a4197395" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354405 1074 "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{15-12}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354029 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354406 1069 "q-85171" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354406 1071 "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354406 1072 #uuid "b177e082-aca8-5ae3-bb5e-4b8f9e1549ff" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354406 1074 "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{18-16}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353875 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354407 1069 "q-85172" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354407 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(10,5),(0,-5),$ and $(15,20).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(9,5)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(10,-7)$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354407 1072 #uuid "9ca71fc4-780f-59ae-bb16-2f59cfd2fbb5" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354407 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{35-30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 50 & -125 \\\\ 5 & -35 & 95\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 & -25 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 19\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(0,1),(10,-7),(-25,19).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353995 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354408 1069 "q-85177" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354408 1071 "![](images/97/97dfdde016e4c3482fe036379e22aa95af27c1bcf93076ea9054e263100ad8e1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354408 1072 #uuid "6f92163d-ae56-5dad-9ed9-3681dc9f9768" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354408 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354094 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354409 1069 "q-85182" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354409 1071 "![](images/51/51439321ae9d3760eb48d54351dbd519efe290c77d9197ca55aa590ce79e91a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354409 1072 #uuid "a399d3dc-47f2-5985-b430-d143a3ff87d1" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354409 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/bf/bf2d20acbb93d3c61e9890a49d1c15a08c2b4e18b474da421bded7cf3dafc297.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354155 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354410 1069 "q-8519" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354410 1071 "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(6,{45}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354410 1072 #uuid "f70a1b51-73cd-5475-aadd-7e2e3bee491f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354410 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate od $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353833 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354411 1069 "q-85204" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354411 1071 "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354411 1072 #uuid "84717eb7-adfd-5852-9f6a-4e3469d4b99e" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354411 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (4-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-12\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-0] \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353923 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354412 1069 "q-85206" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354412 1071 "![](images/bf/bf7089294bc72330065590a9fe54acebee6d0ab982bffb4a162407c7232898ec.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354412 1072 #uuid "e2773090-aaa0-5b0f-ab6e-ecfc1a50c98f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354412 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/1b/1b83e2674bba4bd6c326083f8c94496b5288de23575dc63a92b9a660eba69f67.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354154 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354413 1069 "q-85234" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354413 1071 "![](images/87/87635426224ded01c8bf486c7f59eeb6cacbe4e5c7db964643910bb5d142f0f3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354413 1072 #uuid "5348ffc1-6c19-57de-8e72-a13dbe8b5969" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354413 1074 "![](images/9f/9f64bae56998bca7d825780c8c2b437ae3106486053cc9c8ed1b1fca2a88d4ab.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353955 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354414 1069 "q-85278" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354414 1071 "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354414 1072 #uuid "476850d6-1065-514f-9c2e-38a6ce482b21" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354414 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-7)^{2}}=\\sqrt{114}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354103 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354415 1069 "q-85280" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354415 1071 "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354415 1072 #uuid "3c34a906-4ce9-5f9d-848e-084be407b879" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354415 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents 