ratorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354417 1072 #uuid "0f224d57-600e-5d72-9cfc-422703beddb0" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354417 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{24}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+25)\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-2$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{24}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{24},\\frac{13\\pi }{24}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\cdot \\frac{13\\pi }{24}=\\frac{13{\\pi }^{2}}{576}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354057 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354418 1069 "q-8558" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354418 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354418 1072 #uuid "5c0b1dd8-dc15-5ecc-aa3e-52d0e022d6e2" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354418 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-13)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=5$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=6$ gives $x=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{23\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{23\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+\\frac{23\\pi }{12}=\\frac{28\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353999 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354419 1069 "q-8559" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354419 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3})+\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354419 1072 #uuid "740c307e-eac2-5253-b736-687366a7aaed" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354419 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3})+\\sqrt{3}=0 \\\\ 