f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354011 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354537 1069 "q-86869" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354537 1071 "A railway train has $6$ passenger wagons. How many ways can $6$ conductors be distributed among the wagons if one conductor is assigned to each wagon?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354537 1072 #uuid "1af97a6c-2901-5306-ad12-401b993aaea1" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354537 1074 "There are $6$  conductors to arrange, so there are $6!$ ways of arranging them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6! & =6\\times 5\\times \\times 3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =720.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353931 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354538 1069 "q-86898" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354538 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354538 1072 #uuid "0b045a52-4215-5bdb-9dcf-5641984e3591" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354538 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/23/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353854 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354539 1069 "q-86899" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354539 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-|x|+2$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354539 1072 #uuid "fa4fe275-03b1-57bc-8327-4434c307d887" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354539 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/f3/f3ffc82025f80c357c06e28fcb091c701d53d84821bebd9d2886cf4972b77028.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353994 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354540 1069 "q-8690" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354540 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354540 1072 #uuid "d94df7af-08e6-557f-a3ff-43a92cb58dfc" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354540 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=1.738\\,244…≈1.738\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.738$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.738.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.738 & ≈1.404.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.404.$\n\n![](images/39/392ae25b9a2aa47e5292d7e623922803891b52099d20525f888daca92642ce88.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.404 \\\\  & =4.546\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =4.546-2\\pi  \\\\  & ≈-1.738\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.738\\cdot (-1.738)≈-3\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353996 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354541 1069 "q-86901" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354541 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354541 1072 #uuid "d8661edc-888e-5453-80f8-4bc76c526640" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354541 1074 "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =12 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $12$ | $24$ |" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354071 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354542 1069 "q-86915" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354542 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 2<x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354542 1072 #uuid "b72f07eb-0164-5ad4-8559-715adcc982b1" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354542 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}+\\frac{k}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354173 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354543 1069 "q-86930" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354543 1071 "Given a data set with $20$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=169,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354543 1072 #uuid "bce8189f-1e6b-5606-a621-36abaa7a6a48" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354543 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=20$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=169,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 169≈8.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353965 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354544 1069 "q-86932" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354544 1071 "Given a data set with $23$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354544 1072 #uuid "81127073-3815-575d-a148-cb1ef2bb975d" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354544 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=23$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{23}\\cdot 590≈25.7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353929 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354545 1069 "q-86947" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354545 1071 "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=3.5$ intersects the positive $y$-axis." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354545 1072 #uuid "3ab7847b-1891-54e9-9c41-f389a5063166" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354545 1074 "The polar curve $r=3.5$ is a circle of radius $3.5$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point where $r=3.5$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc12f535f40d75a21fc367bf6e08cde9f4ec086c4f358e4ab198a6f44da1aac4.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(3.5,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354053 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354546 1069 "q-86951" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354546 1071 "Given that the mean of the following data set is $8,$ find the value of $w:$ \n\n$$\n7,\\,w,\\,8,\\,5,\\,11,\\,(w-1)\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354546 1072 #uuid "9dd5181b-3e4d-53db-92bc-b711323fed87" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354546 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =7+w+8+5+11+(w-1) \\\\  & =30+2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (30+2w) \\\\  & =\\frac{30+2w}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =\\frac{30+2w}{6} \\\\ 48 & =30+2w \\\\ 18 & =2w \\\\ w & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354204 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354547 1069 "q-86954" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354547 1071 "![](images/ab/abfda82949c8ed899902e142ffab0119b734d583b299e707270e293a24a9be1b.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(5,2)$, as shown above." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354547 1072 #uuid "5b0c8fee-92b7-59e0-9eb2-2a54711f6d83" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354547 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{29}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354047 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354548 1069 "q-86963" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354548 1071 "Given that the mean of the following data set is $4,$ find the value of $z:$ \n\n$$\n2,\\,z,\\,z,\\,6,\\,9,\\,z,\\,2\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354548 1072 #uuid "c417f647-fc55-5614-ab11-76881a9d5af5" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354548 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=7$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} & =2+z+z+6+9+z+2 \\\\  & =19+3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (19+3z) \\\\  & =\\frac{19+3z}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{19+3z}{7} \\\\ 28 & =19+3z \\\\ 9 & =3z \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354231 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354549 1069 "q-86973" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354549 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+5y,\\,y(-3)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=3?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354549 1072 #uuid "9a1894cf-2970-5e7d-8569-0b9fd3dce386" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354549 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(-3)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x+5y \\\\  & =-(-3)+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $24$ |" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354105 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354550 1069 "q-87085" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354550 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354550 1072 #uuid "270879ff-a37d-587e-9313-78b8da6205c1" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354550 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/16/16b0fe5e7b15bf87b41374bf20c8f68e67e960c13fcfd7049e5fde5b51c268e1.png)" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354183 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354551 1069 "q-87125" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354551 1071 "![](images/c0/c010f583fc33ee3c5335c16c0aaf1c20c2e5cf82d49fe7cf14e8e5661bf202fc.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354551 1072 #uuid "ae386b7a-b983-5861-a09e-2627510a22f7" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354551 1074 "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354130 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354552 1069 "q-87127" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354552 1071 "![](images/93/930cb433bd5f972f8b6f7ae12cb937fa84a2f8f30dff9c97f1282a2ead10c3fb.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354552 1072 #uuid "ddee5ee8-ce68-5f22-aeb7-fdb74cce576b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354552 1074 "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n2\\pi -\\frac{2\\pi }{7}=\\frac{12\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{12\\pi }{7}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353974 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354553 1069 "q-87135" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354553 1071 "![](images/55/5507850932bb4061cd19cdbffa18aacf2b235293e643170ee78c4326a96e050d.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(9,-3\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354553 1072 #uuid "6cac9b7a-cc06-5417-92c4-34fbb9bd0872" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354553 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{9}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354198 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354554 1069 "q-87139" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354554 1071 "![](images/38/386a4b395d45276284606b0f079be16881af717ba7e791ab62d8df34743f0269.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354554 1072 #uuid "91695729-12cb-52c7-8253-928e6e7c6bec" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354554 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353868 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354555 1069 "q-8715" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354555 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354555 1072 #uuid "66e86d42-68bb-5bd3-bc77-fc004d21b719" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354555 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi +0=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=0,\\pi .$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354025 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354556 1069 "q-87151" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354556 1071 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped twice\n- The height of a randomly selected person, measured in meters\n- The concentration, in grams per cubic centimeter, of glucose in a person's bloodstream" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354556 1072 #uuid "72b0b871-668d-5ed4-92c2-5e17bf34749d" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354556 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354237 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354557 1069 "q-87153" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354557 1071 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The amount of water per month consumed by a randomly selected person\n- The number of pages in a particular book\n- The amount of time that a randomly selected person spends waiting for a particular bus" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354557 1072 #uuid "3d1bc0bc-e996-5273-b3cb-33c77433dc02" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354557 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of pages in a particular book is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354210 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354558 1069 "q-87155" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354558 1071 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The score when a roulette wheel is spun at random\n- The amount of water consumed by a randomly selected person on a particular day, measured in liters\n- The number of students in a particular class on a randomly selected day" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354558 1072 #uuid "404cdbaf-9eb2-5b1a-bb37-f06578b6497c" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354558 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible outcomes do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354101 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354559 1069 "q-87160" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354559 1071 "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume of milk produced by a particular cow on a randomly selected day, measured in liters\n- The outcome of rolling a $6$-sided die\n- The distance that a randomly selected runner can run in $30$ minutes" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354559 1072 #uuid "34701839-8ff2-5e14-88d5-939b8e4e71de" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354559 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354138 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354560 1069 "q-8720" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354560 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354560 1072 #uuid "0b353be4-34cb-5496-83c4-11d3f5d0b538" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354560 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-\\pi +{x}_{1}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354045 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354561 1069 "q-87219" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354561 1071 "Compute $P(X>6)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354561 1072 #uuid "037cbc64-bf8a-5ac9-8eee-6e588cecbf91" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354561 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(6<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-3x}]}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354125 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354562 1069 "q-8723" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354562 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions, in radians, of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-2$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value to $3$ decimal places of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354562 1072 #uuid "69d2b0a5-9cb2-5b84-9fe6-c4054e3089c5" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354562 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)=-1.107\\,148...≈-1.107\\,149\n$$\n\nSince $-1.107\\,149$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-1.107\\,149.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -1.107\\,148=2.034\\,444.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-1.107\\,149,2.034\\,444.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.107\\,149\\cdot 2.034\\,444≈-2.252\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353894 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354563 1069 "q-87236" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354563 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}},\\, & 3<x<6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354563 1072 #uuid "7c5dab49-c074-55ae-82a5-34331ee5b508" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354563 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}k{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{72}+\\frac{k}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{24}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354136 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354564 1069 "q-87238" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354564 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{-5x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354564 1072 #uuid "ecbd7594-c780-5122-b4fc-c144d917449d" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354564 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-5x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{5}{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=5.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353949 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354565 1069 "q-87240" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354565 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{6x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354565 1072 #uuid "aa97e0b6-e6e8-55fc-97fe-a3748ab626dc" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354565 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{6x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x\\le 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x={[\\frac{k}{6}{e}^{6x}]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353851 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354566 1069 "q-87253" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354566 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $15$ data points, where \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=-30,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}^{2}=120.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354566 1072 #uuid "5df6abca-76bb-5ea7-98d2-52a80505aaef" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354566 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=15$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=-30,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot (-30) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}^{2}=120,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =8-{(-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353911 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354567 1069 "q-87262" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354567 1071 "Given that $P(A)=0.5,$ $P(A\\cap B)=0.4,$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.8,$ find $P({B}^{′}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354567 1072 #uuid "98c1f989-8fe6-518d-bf14-b7c905f21857" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354567 1074 "First, we calculate $P(B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.8 & =0.5+P(B)-0.4 \\\\ P(B) & =0.8+0.4-0.5 \\\\ P(B) & =0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({B}^{′}) & =1-P(B) \\\\  & =1-0.7 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353866 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354568 1069 "q-87286" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354568 1071 "Given $P(A)=\\frac{2}{7},$ $P(B)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{10}{21},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354568 1072 #uuid "3b577de5-3225-5087-b612-28eaf461d127" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354568 1074 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{10}{21} & =\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{1}{3}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{10}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+7-10}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354152 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354569 1069 "q-87294" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354569 1071 "Given $P(A)=\\frac{1}{3}$, $P(B)=\\frac{3}{8}$, $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{17}{24}$, determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354569 1072 #uuid "7ec9ff46-b349-5ee9-a464-63c7e6c25eec" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354569 1074 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{17}{24} & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{17}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{8+9-17}{24}=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353930 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354570 1069 "q-87322" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354570 1071 "Factor ${y}^{10}+16.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354570 1072 #uuid "c14885ac-0ffb-5a3d-b46f-bfeeee6a4d0b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354570 1074 "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{y}^{10}+16={({y}^{5})}^{2}+{4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{10}+16 & ={y}^{10}-(-16) \\\\  & ={y}^{10}-(16{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({y}^{5})}^{2}-{(4\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})({y}^{5}-4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353985 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354571 1069 "q-87324" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354571 1071 "Factor ${x}^{4}+9.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354571 1072 #uuid "08c6a99b-5f95-55ac-a2dd-08dc4afc35e8" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354571 1074 "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+9={({x}^{2})}^{2}+{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+9 & ={x}^{4}-(-9) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}-{(3\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3\\text{i})({x}^{2}-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353914 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354572 1069 "q-87327" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354572 1071 "Factor $25{a}^{8}+1.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354572 1072 #uuid "cbffddb1-a81e-57b3-9d00-21ae9889e9c4" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354572 1074 "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n25{a}^{8}+1={(5{a}^{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{a}^{8}+1 & =25{a}^{8}-(-1) \\\\  & =25{a}^{8}-({\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(5{a}^{4})}^{2}-{(\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =(5{a}^{4}+\\text{i})(5{a}^{4}-\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354153 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354573 1069 "q-87387" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354573 1071 "If $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)\\ge 0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354573 1072 #uuid "9839eaa3-e959-590f-b517-4d3c93cc178d" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354573 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)\\ge 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+1)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{2}$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1,$  where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/e6/e6aa75d55f2776e3e2b5d029ad51ad9e39a80e49b613705a97fa01ff34113db5.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)\\ge 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup {0}\\cup [1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354014 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354574 1069 "q-87450" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354574 1071 "Solve $3(7-4x)^{2}+5<-25.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354574 1072 #uuid "d7039847-1b69-52b3-8dac-7ae33b0df937" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354574 1074 "Isolating the quadratic term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(7-4x)^{2}+5 & <-25 \\\\ 3(7-4x)^{2} & <-30 \\\\ (7-4x)^{2} & <-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $(7-4x)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(2)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353968 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354575 1069 "q-87461" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354575 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n3,\\,1,\\,15,\\,3,\\,2,\\,12\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354575 1072 #uuid "7b280e79-95a2-5f09-8e97-f2a9148deaac" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354575 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (3+1+15+3+2+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(3-6)^{2}+(1-6)^{2}+(15-6)^{2}+(3-6)^{2}+(2-6)^{2}+(12-6)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[9+25+81+9+16+36] \\\\  & =\\frac{176}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{88}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354035 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354576 1069 "q-87464" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354576 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n-3,\\,1,\\,-5,\\,-5,\\,2,\\,-8\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354576 1072 #uuid "a33c9758-e856-52e3-9ca0-8b4d2b8f793f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354576 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-3+1-5-5+2-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(-3-(-3))^{2}+(1-(-3))^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}+(2-(-3))^{2}+(-8-(-3))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[0+16+4+4+25+25] \\\\  & =\\frac{74}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353967 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354577 1069 "q-8747" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354577 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354577 1072 #uuid "6ed802b3-b056-5f83-8a03-3932e7ced220" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354577 1074 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354020 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354578 1069 "q-87502" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354578 1071 "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(1,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354578 1072 #uuid "5c3f9e3b-ce11-530a-9baa-05d3b323a845" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354578 1074 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n \n\n![](images/05/052b405c104f0d6bc353782c6e07bfb1e0245726d86346a545ea38aa31941711.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(1,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-2))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354017 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354579 1069 "q-87515" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354579 1071 "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(2,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354579 1072 #uuid "b28c6e16-047f-5299-b8dc-57e7d48491a3" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354579 1074 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/8b/8be39e6dde084154ecfd43718cb76352c69d26bd76d520177a6c41ee69a7f18e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(2,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-4y+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-8x+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4y+20 \\\\ 8y & =8x-16 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(x-2)^{2}-4x-4\\cdot (x-2)+8 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+4-4x-4x+8+8 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-12x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(3)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(3,1).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353927 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354580 1069 "q-87521" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354580 1071 "Given the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)^{2}(1-x),$ find the solution of $P(x)<0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354580 1072 #uuid "63a0a817-e600-57af-8512-afc6b21072be" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354580 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)^{2}(1-x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ where\n\n- $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=1$ is a simple root.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a negative leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/93/9380e0f00b0df89a4148aa184ad73a1edfad8b76c9755f23926d6b856fdddb62.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)<0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is below the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353852 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354581 1069 "q-87555" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354581 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $5$ data points, where \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=-20,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}^{2}=170.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354581 1072 #uuid "b5f2ca31-7791-579a-a1bd-93c11b023e65" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354581 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=-20,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}^{2}=170,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 170 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =34-{(-4)}^{2} \\\\  & =34-16 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354076 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354582 1069 "q-87564" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354582 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $7$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=35,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=201.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354582 1072 #uuid "eb6661e4-f7f8-5234-a154-aa0158afed89" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354582 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=35,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=201,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 201 \\\\  & =\\frac{201}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{201}{7}-{(5)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{201}{7}-25 \\\\  & =\\frac{201-175}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354063 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354583 1069 "q-87602" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354583 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $8$ data points, where \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=-56,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=560.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354583 1072 #uuid "3b43ca6c-4a0d-5c5b-8aa1-c1e03274a8b6" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354583 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points 