194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354591 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{4-3x}-2\\le 0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354591 1072 #uuid "32040622-1ccf-514b-8e21-c079e05e8f01" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354591 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{4-3x}-2 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{4-3x} & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{4-3x})}^{3} & \\le {2}^{3} \\\\ 4-3x & \\le 8 \\\\ -3x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we had to flip the sign of the inequality because we divided by a negative number.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353920 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354592 1069 "q-87672" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354592 1071 "Given $P(x)=12{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-{x}^{4},$ solve $P(x)>0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354592 1072 #uuid "7c00bb6a-813c-59fa-acb9-a9cec9626eaa" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354592 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =12{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-12) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+3x-12) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}(x(x-4)+3(x-4)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}(x+3)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=12{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=4,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-3$ and $x=4$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/5e/5ebfbd4e18de00fad05c4192be2147a9b9765e00fae5b74ac608e85466c21e73.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,4).\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353977 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354593 1069 "q-87681" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354593 1071 "Solve the inequality $3-\\sqrt{x-1}<8.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354593 1072 #uuid "83f0a333-2fcc-590c-814f-2381d3593f36" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354593 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\sqrt{x-1} & <8 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x-1} & <5 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-1} & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-5),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx-1\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge 1.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353941 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354594 1069 "q-87682" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354594 1071 "Solve the inequality $4-3\\sqrt{x+3}\\le 10.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354594 1072 #uuid "9074503a-7fbc-5fac-ae9b-2f9f6fc0d5dc" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354594 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3\\sqrt{x+3} & \\le 10 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{x+3} & \\le 6 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3} & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-2),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx+3\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge -3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -3.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354064 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354595 1069 "q-87685" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354595 1071 "Solve the inequality $1-4\\sqrt{3x-2}\\le 9.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354595 1072 #uuid "da31cea1-12f6-5f74-88b0-155a9da4a07b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354595 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4\\sqrt{3x-2} & \\le 9 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{3x-2} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x-2} & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-2),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n3x-2\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354004 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354596 1069 "q-87731" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354596 1071 "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4}-3\\le 1.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354596 1072 #uuid "6b5bd617-9c96-55be-9221-807f1cd9f7c9" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354596 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4}-3 & \\le 1 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4} & \\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x+4}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ (x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ (x+4)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ -(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & \\ge -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354251 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354597 1069 "q-87781" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354597 1071 "Solve the inequality $2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13\\le -5.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354597 1072 #uuid "9fc08219-8cf5-5739-9502-636c289d32a3" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354597 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13 & \\le -5 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(4).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4x+3})}^{2} & \\le {4}^{2} \\\\ 4x+3 & \\le 16 \\\\ 4x & \\le 13 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{13}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{3}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}]\\cap [-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )=[-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354168 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354598 1069 "q-87782" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354598 1071 "Solve the inequality $12-5\\sqrt{3x+2}\\le 2.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354598 1072 #uuid "b69bc8b6-ff1c-556f-a4d8-037531c1b6a8" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354598 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-5\\sqrt{3x+2} & \\le 2 \\\\ -5\\sqrt{3x+2} & \\le -10 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+2} & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+2})}^{2} & \\ge {2}^{2} \\\\ 3x+2 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -2 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{2}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )\\cap [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )=[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354126 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354599 1069 "q-87801" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354599 1071 "![](images/f1/f18be4b42008423fe08b165fea4b3807b247813112b92a6ad0932c07a33ba8b6.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354599 1072 #uuid "b779fa51-4da1-5c93-b91d-35c3acfc0806" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354599 1074 "Since $m∠A=m∠C$, we have an isosceles triangle, where $AB=BC.$\n\nHence, the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354019 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354600 1069 "q-87802" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354600 1071 "![](images/37/3756b00cce29aad77b7a0b1ebb09f43cb89a602f8462edce89e8ba6346cb7fab.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354600 1072 #uuid "298d5629-725b-5aa5-9d76-4ed61398d66b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354600 1074 "Since $m∠A=m∠B$, we have an isosceles triangle, where $AC=BC.$\n\nHence, the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353908 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354601 1069 "q-87821" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354601 1071 "Solve the inequality $3(2-5x)^{9}+5\\le 8.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354601 1072 #uuid "41fb7c84-f5cd-5461-8878-c048d20e2296" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354601 1074 "Isolating the ninth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2-5x)^{9}+5 & \\le 8 \\\\ 3(2-5x)^{9} & \\le 3 \\\\ (2-5x)^{9} & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the ninth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[9]{(2-5x)^{9}} & \\le \\sqrt[9]{1} \\\\ 2-5x & \\le 1 \\\\ -5x & \\le -1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354003 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354602 1069 "q-87827" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354602 1071 "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}\\le 4.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354602 1072 #uuid "e2131549-b481-5f3d-ab57-c0250499cdd1" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354602 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le 4 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354241 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354603 1069 "q-87828" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354603 1071 "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}<25.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354603 1072 #uuid "653d874d-a486-580e-a2d8-67e03055d454" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354603 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <25 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354214 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354604 1069 "q-87831" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354604 1071 "Solve the inequality ${(0.72)}^{x}-4\\le 0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354604 1072 #uuid "f2729c48-f673-5053-a5a6-d9fd97484f1b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354604 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.72)}^{x}-4 & \\le 0 \\\\ {(0.72)}^{x} & \\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.72)^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.72) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.72)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.72)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354232 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354605 1069 "q-87833" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354605 1071 "Solve the inequality ${(0.84)}^{x+5}-20>0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354605 1072 #uuid "408c73d7-77ed-5048-8b0f-5ed08b5ed26e" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354605 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.84)}^{x+5}-20 & >0 \\\\ {(0.84)}^{x+5} & >20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.84)^{x+5}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20) \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)} \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354199 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354606 1069 "q-87834" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354606 1071 "Solve $(x-3)^{6}+54>-10.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354606 1072 #uuid "7a3e7b21-de1c-5ade-a143-468639e960ad" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354606 1074 "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{6}+54 & >-10 \\\\ (x-3)^{6} & >-64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $(x-3)^{6}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(6)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the given inequality is all real numbers." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354156 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354607 1069 "q-87835" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354607 1071 "Solve $(4-3x)^{8}+12<9.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354607 1072 #uuid "62ccc2af-c132-56e8-a955-5888b04ab83f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354607 1074 "Isolating the eighth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-3x)^{8}+12 & <9 \\\\ (4-3x)^{8} & <-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $(4-3x)^{8}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(8)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354041 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354608 1069 "q-87837" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354608 1071 "Solve $2(x+7)^{2}+4\\le -6.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354608 1072 #uuid "22ced630-8e66-5c9a-9b06-87ae388e3942" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354608 1074 "Isolating the quadratic term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+7)^{2}+4 & \\le -6 \\\\ 2(x+7)^{2} & \\le -10 \\\\ (x+7)^{2} & \\le -5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $(x+7)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(2)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354265 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354609 1069 "q-87840" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354609 1071 "Solve $4(2-x)^{8}+3\\ge -9.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354609 1072 #uuid "96d6c106-d8cd-51b8-9aea-6ed514956340" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354609 1074 "Isolating the eighth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2-x)^{8}+3 & \\ge -9 \\\\ 4(2-x)^{8} & \\ge -12 \\\\ (2-x)^{8} & \\ge -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $(2-x)^{8}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(8)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the given inequality is all real numbers." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353878 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354610 1069 "q-87853" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354610 1071 "![](images/16/1616f8d921cbdaf1a5654354292e261b0941e5dbfcee16ae620c8a1cf3e92135.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354610 1072 #uuid "2e4dded3-cef3-55b9-ba8c-e50ddf3e7619" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354610 1074 "Since $m∠A=m∠C={45}^{∘}$ and $AB=BC,$ we have an isosceles right  triangle. \n\n![](images/3f/3f9a52834fe76f1560beec8580dbd91076a9f7c6ef980f410aa5bfe7da162c9c.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {20}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 400 \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354191 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354611 1069 "q-87854" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354611 1071 "![](images/7a/7ab7df3df1802d469239de4d3fd7ae7660fc449f807290c0e65a6db7d565d233.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354611 1072 #uuid "28c2231f-e13d-5012-b0b7-8b270bc4ee92" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354611 1074 "Since $m∠A=m∠C={45}^{∘}$ and $AB=BC,$ we have an isosceles right triangle. \n\n![](images/21/218d08d1f46e608c5fb7d1c2f5ddd136e601e15514dc829c686b35ff42b15a8f.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {9}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 81 \\\\  & =40.5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353928 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354612 1069 "q-87855" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354612 1071 "Solve the inequality $9\\cdot {6}^{x}\\ge {7}^{1-x}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354612 1072 #uuid "31b56957-e22a-5130-a991-cda2765335d4" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354612 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot {6}^{x} & \\ge {7}^{1-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(9\\cdot {6}^{x}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{1-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡9+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{x}) & \\ge (1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡9+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354244 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354613 1069 "q-87872" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354613 1071 "Solve the inequality $4-{e}^{x+1}>6.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354613 1072 #uuid "5a818427-f56a-5b99-aa8a-d9578edcba8f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354613 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-{e}^{x+1} & >6 \\\\ -{e}^{x+1} & >2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{e}^{x+1}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${e}^{x+1}<-2$ has no solutions." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354093 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354614 1069 "q-87873" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354614 1071 "Solve the inequality $-{e}^{3x-2}>15.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354614 1072 #uuid "d479595a-0013-5766-89ed-2d959da7a96f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354614 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3x-2} & >15 \\\\ {e}^{3x-2} & <-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x-2}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${e}^{3x-2}<-15$ has no solutions." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353882 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354615 1069 "q-87874" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354615 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{5}-{e}^{x-2}>\\frac{4}{5}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354615 1072 #uuid "9dd643f7-3e75-5c7e-a93c-c2eb5b57b6c4" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354615 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}-{e}^{x-2} & >\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -{e}^{x-2} & >\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{e}^{x-2}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${e}^{x-2}<-\\frac{3}{5}$ has no solutions." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354206 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354616 1069 "q-87876" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354616 1071 "Solve the inequality $-4-{e}^{3x}>2.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354616 1072 #uuid "48ad6fff-2c0d-5121-b124-5aea1ae6b3d8" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354616 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-{e}^{3x} & >2 \\\\ -{e}^{3x} & >6 \\\\ {e}^{3x} & <-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $e>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${e}^{3x}$ cannot be less than $-6.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}<-6\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353982 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354617 1069 "q-8789" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354617 1071 "![](images/37/37a9d6fca5d60e8f614730a121e1f5da23011e6c7a8ea80840a16b81e376638b.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=10,$ shown above." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354617 1072 #uuid "567b0625-ac08-5240-8a74-33cef6f5facb" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354617 1074 "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(0+2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+4 & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =4\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(4-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(4+\\sqrt{6},0).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354218 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354618 1069 "q-87891" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354618 1071 "Solve the inequality $3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}+27>0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354618 1072 #uuid "a2e7a876-0e47-5bf9-934f-c1867c02a503" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354618 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}+27 & >0 \\\\ 3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x} & >-27 \\\\ {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x} & >-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $\\frac{15}{4}>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}$ is greater than $-9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}>-9\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353823 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354619 1069 "q-87895" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354619 1071 "Solve the inequality $7\\cdot {4}^{1-x}<{3}^{x}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354619 1072 #uuid "aa6e2ccf-0693-503e-8461-0b4e95574681" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354619 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot {4}^{1-x} & <{3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot {4}^{1-x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡7+(1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353922 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354620 1069 "q-87908" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354620 1071 "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {2}^{x}<{3}^{2-x}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354620 1072 #uuid "816dc271-5ddd-5677-9ed2-4f251f36f649" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354620 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{x} & <{3}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {2}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & <2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & <2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354060 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354621 1069 "q-87918" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354621 1071 "Solve the inequality $(5-x)^{2}+4\\le 6.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354621 1072 #uuid "be4bafeb-6346-5a86-8f29-98f25656b707" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354621 1074 "Isolating the quadratic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-x)^{2}+4 & \\le 6 \\\\ (5-x)^{2} & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(5-x)^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{2} \\\\ |5-x| & \\le \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{2}\\le 5-x\\le \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\le 5-x & \\le \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}-5\\le -x & \\le \\sqrt{2}-5 \\\\ 5+\\sqrt{2}\\ge x & \\ge 5-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the lower number is on the left:\n\n$$\n5-\\sqrt{2}\\le x\\le 5+\\sqrt{2}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353932 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354622 1069 "q-87920" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354622 1071 "Solve the inequality $2(x-2)^{6}-1\\ge 3.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354622 1072 #uuid "aeba79aa-fe89-5581-9a94-26c725e2679f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354622 1074 "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-2)^{6}-1 & \\ge 3 \\\\ 2(x-2)^{6} & \\ge 4 \\\\ (x-2)^{6} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the sixth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{(x-2)^{6}} & \\ge \\sqrt[6]{2} \\\\ |x-2| & \\ge \\sqrt[6]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\nx-2\\le -\\sqrt[6]{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x-2\\ge \\sqrt[6]{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\le 2-\\sqrt[6]{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2+\\sqrt[6]{2}.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354258 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354623 1069 "q-8794" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354623 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercept of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1=0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354623 1072 #uuid "591b47fb-ebcd-5f48-9b95-6d9ef3025f87" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354623 1074 "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4\\cdot 0-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercept occurs at the point $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/87766af5840b6b3729fa200d383e18c5f7926d9000633f99b481e8b4a6922732.png)" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353966 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354624 1069 "q-8795" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354624 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x+4y=5.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354624 1072 #uuid "0fcd13ca-d52b-51d2-bdaa-de1533b828f6" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354624 1074 "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x+4y & =5 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-0+4y & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =5+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,-5)$ and $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e4f7c975282aee647b2fa161ba34abac5c19e6bc3fc68f812533a0eb527834a.png)" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353870 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354625 1069 "q-88004" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354625 1071 "Solve the inequality $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+12\\ge 0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354625 1072 #uuid "95eea71c-7bf8-5863-9cc1-a92906e34c7e" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354625 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+12 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & \\ge {e}^{-3} \\\\ x+1 & \\ge {e}^{-3} \\\\ x & \\ge {e}^{-3}-1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+1>0\\,⇒\\,x>-1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=[\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{{e}^{3}}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353918 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354626 1069 "q-88005" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354626 1071 "Solve the inequality $7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+28<0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354626 1072 #uuid "c3e95f69-6f53-5efd-b625-33df0fcacf41" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354626 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+28 & <0 \\\\ 7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & <-28 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)} & <{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2x & <{e}^{-4} \\\\ x & <\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2} \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n2x>0\\,⇒\\,x>0\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(0,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}})\\cap (0,\\infty )=(0,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}),$ which can also be written as $0<x<\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354257 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354627 1069 "q-88006" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354627 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+4\\ge 0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354627 1072 #uuid "03f5c85f-7df7-57a2-a308-f1fc35e5399c" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354627 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & \\ge -4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge {e}^{-6} \\\\ x-2 & \\ge {e}^{-6} \\\\ x & \\ge {e}^{-6}+2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-2>0\\,⇒\\,x>2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353834 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354628 1069 "q-88011" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354628 1071 "Solve the inequality $4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)-16<0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354628 1072 #uuid "6638bc35-3c65-5e74-96ea-a57758d59a30" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354628 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)-16 & <0 \\\\ 4{log}_{4}⁡(x+2) & <16 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(x+2) & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $4.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x+2)} & <{4}^{4} \\\\ x+2 & <256 \\\\ x & <254\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⇒\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<254,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,254).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,254)\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(-2,254)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-2,254),$ which can also be written as $-2<x<254.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354189 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354629 1069 "q-88016" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354629 1071 "Solve the inequality $4{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)-\\frac{4}{3}>0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354629 1072 #uuid "891d2699-d957-58a5-a0e7-8438c3ccd70b" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354629 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)-\\frac{4}{3} & >0 \\\\ 4{log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & >\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & >\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $7.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)} & >{7}^{1/3} \\\\ x+1 & >\\sqrt[3]{7} \\\\ x & >\\sqrt[3]{7}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+1>0\\,⇒\\,x>-1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,$ which can be expressed as $(\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\sqrt[3]{7}-1.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353954 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354630 1069 "q-88017" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354630 1071 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(x-3)+3>{log}_{2}⁡(4x).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354630 1072 #uuid "077b4f54-4b78-5ded-9b5e-a2fac66f7469" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354630 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-3)+3} & >{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x)} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-3)}\\cdot {2}^{3} & >{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x)} \\\\ (x-3)\\cdot 8 & >4x \\\\ 8x-24 & >4x \\\\ 4x & >24 \\\\ x & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >3 \\\\ 4x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>6,$ which can be expressed as $(6,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>3$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(3,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(6,\\infty )\\cap (3,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=(6,\\infty )\n$$\n\n Therefore, the solution is $x\\in (6,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>6.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354115 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354631 1069 "q-88019" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354631 1071 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{4}⁡(x-2)+1\\le {log}_{4}⁡(x+1).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354631 1072 #uuid "8bb84421-055c-5b80-b7d1-3ef221b401e9" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354631 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $4.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x-2)+1} & \\le {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x+1)} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x-2)}\\cdot {4}^{1} & \\le {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(x+1)} \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot 4 & \\le x+1 \\\\ 4x-8 & \\le x+1 \\\\ 3x & \\le 9 \\\\ x & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2 \\\\ x+1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 3,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,3].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>2$ and $x>-1,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty )$ and $(-1,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3]\\cap (2,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(2,3]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,3],$ which can also be written as $2<x\\le 3.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354196 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354632 1069 "q-88022" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354632 1071 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{5}⁡(6x)+2\\ge {log}_{5}⁡(3x)+3.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354632 1072 #uuid "5bd8adb9-56e1-506e-99bd-ae316eb9aec4" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354632 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $5.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(6x)+2} & \\ge {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(3x)+3} \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(6x)}\\cdot {5}^{2} & \\ge {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\ 6x\\cdot 25 & \\ge 3x\\cdot 125 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 5x \\\\ -3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0 \\\\ 3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 0,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,0].$ Additionally, the domain of both logarithms is $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(0,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0]\\cap (0,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354051 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354633 1069 "q-88024" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354633 1071 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(x+3)+4\\le {log}_{2}⁡(x-2).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354633 1072 #uuid "ce3e7fef-efaf-5f88-98aa-9f5c7569d3a3" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354633 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+3)+4} & \\le {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+3)}\\cdot {2}^{4} & \\le {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ (x+3)\\cdot 16 & \\le x-2 \\\\ 16x+48 & \\le x-2 \\\\ 15x & \\le -50 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-3 \\\\ x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le -\\frac{10}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{10}{3}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-3$ and $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,\\infty )$ and $(2,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{10}{3}]\\cap (-3,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354023 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354634 1069 "q-88037" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354634 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)-2\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354634 1072 #uuid "fa19ac45-0a11-528b-a418-f784c1cbab53" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354634 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)-2} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\\cdot {e}^{-2} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} \\\\ (x-1){e}^{-2} & \\ge 5x \\\\ x-1 & \\ge 5{e}^{2}x \\\\ -1 & \\ge (5{e}^{2}-1)x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1} & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >1 \\\\ 5x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le -\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>1$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1}]\\cap (1,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354099 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354635 1069 "q-88040" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354635 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-4<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354635 1072 #uuid "faaad791-07b8-54a1-b99b-01e03ce1f480" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354635 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-4} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}\\cdot {e}^{-4} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)} \\\\ (x+1){e}^{-4} & <x-1 \\\\ (x+1) & <(x-1){e}^{4} \\\\ x+1 & <x{e}^{4}-{e}^{4} \\\\ 1+{e}^{4} & <x({e}^{4}-1) \\\\ \\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1} & <x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-1 \\\\ x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-1$ and $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,\\infty )$ and $(1,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\frac{1+{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-1}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353944 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354636 1069 "q-88041" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354636 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+5.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354636 1072 #uuid "ed8de50f-e70c-58d6-9321-a5a5723ada29" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354636 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} & \\le {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+5} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} & \\le {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\\cdot {e}^{5} \\\\ 3x & \\le (x-1){e}^{5} \\\\ 3x & \\le {e}^{5}x-{e}^{5} \\\\ {e}^{5} & \\le ({e}^{5}-3)x \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>0$ and $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(0,\\infty )$ and $(1,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=[\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353884 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354637 1069 "q-8820" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354637 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6x+4y=1 \\\\ x-8y=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354637 1072 #uuid "302f133a-6090-5037-99fd-6973d6637f17" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354637 1074 "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6x+4y=1 \\\\ x-8y=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354075 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354638 1069 "q-88208" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354638 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354638 1072 #uuid "02a56550-64c7-56dd-a9e8-7fca3f195d30" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354638 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353913 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354639 1069 "q-88228" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354639 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{2x-1}<-8{e}^{2x-1}-6x{e}^{2x-1}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354639 1072 #uuid "0ae10e66-ed82-5f0a-bb92-aac07078f1a2" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354639 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x-1}<-8{e}^{2x-1}-6x{e}^{2x-1} \\\\ {x}^{2}{e}^{2x-1}+6x{e}^{2x-1}+8{e}^{2x-1} & <0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+6x+8){e}^{2x-1} & <0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+2){e}^{2x-1} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${e}^{2x-1}$ and get\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x+2)<0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis when $-4<x<-2.$\n\n![](images/15/15a31e60a938b604b96ade5e6868c658a2e2f0a53fe2a5f53a032093155e8aee.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-4<x<-2.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354016 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354640 1069 "q-88239" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354640 1071 "Solve the inequality $-2x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x}\\ge -{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354640 1072 #uuid "82a54120-1902-56b9-afd2-79670e101fc8" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354640 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & \\ge -{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {x}^{2}{e}^{5x}-2x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-3){e}^{5x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3){e}^{5x} & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${e}^{5x}$ and get\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-3)\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ The parabola is above the $x$-axis when $x\\le -1$ or $x\\ge 3.$\n\n![](images/6a/6aca0f7c055a5f326c2d0d3bc65b6e68a90c8cb496bc1934dbae6093d0d71f96.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -1$ or $x\\ge 3.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354159 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354641 1069 "q-88266" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354641 1071 "Given that $B=[\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(B).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354641 1072 #uuid "af3f4732-8a8f-51ae-b35b-754f1abeb181" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354641 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(24-(-5))\\cdot (-14-(-12)) \\\\  & =29\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-58.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353895 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354642 1069 "q-88296" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354642 1071 "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354642 1072 #uuid "31688145-09ed-5774-a522-d76e627c7dee" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354642 1074 "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353997 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354643 1069 "q-88298" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354643 1071 "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354643 1072 #uuid "27889ca3-5f85-5eb8-aee5-93c6c3d6c2ad" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354643 1074 "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354037 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354644 1069 "q-88300" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354644 1071 "Solve the inequality $-{3}^{6x+1}x<-{3}^{6x}{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354644 1072 #uuid "96bf8bb8-3cef-52a5-bb39-7b68f7f0e796" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354644 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{3}^{6x+1}x & <-{3}^{6x}{x}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{6x}{x}^{2}-{3}^{6x+1}x & <0 \\\\ {3}^{6x}({x}^{2}-3x) & <0 \\\\ {3}^{6x}x(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{6x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x-3)<0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis when $0<x<3.$\n\n![](images/85/8582e3a512cc29618a3e2db9dda4478481167eefcf0a2fbdfb7d33e5786ba62e.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is  $0<x<3.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354180 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354645 1069 "q-88301" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354645 1071 "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354645 1072 #uuid "26f8d807-4854-5fae-88e0-bb503698def0" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354645 1074 "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 92 & 93\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 93-6\\cdot 92) \\\\  & =-(279-552) \\\\  & =273\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354089 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354646 1069 "q-88304" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354646 1071 "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354646 1072 #uuid "da76c31c-efa0-5055-96f9-ec1ecc76273f" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354646 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $1$st row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 99-2\\cdot 98 \\\\  & =396-196 \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353910 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354647 1069 "q-8831" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354647 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354647 1072 #uuid "44ebc4c9-5f5b-5d3f-9b4d-791bbaf01aa4" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354647 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-5 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})=1.190\\,289\\,949…≈1.190\\,290\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $1.190\\,290$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.190\\,290.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +1.190\\,289\\,949…=4.331\\,882\\,603\\,…≈4.331\\,883\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.190\\,290\\cdot 4.331\\,883≈5.156\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354219 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354648 1069 "q-88311" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354648 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{7x}\\ge {5}^{7x-3}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354648 1072 #uuid "4dddb2a1-bad8-5a58-bf61-06f4e359cc2c" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354648 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{7x} & \\ge {5}^{7x-3} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{7x}-{5}^{7x-3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ ({x}^{3}-{5}^{-3})\\cdot {5}^{7x} & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${5}^{7x}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{5}^{-3}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve this inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & \\ge {5}^{-3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}} & \\ge \\sqrt[3]{{5}^{-3}} \\\\ x & \\ge {({5}^{-3})}^{1/3} \\\\ x & \\ge {5}^{-1} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x\\ge \\frac{1}{5}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354118 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354649 1069 "q-8835" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354649 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354649 1072 #uuid "a377bb9f-3f2c-5287-a1a3-0b3492e46cfb" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354649 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{4\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+4 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{4}{3})=-0.927\\,295\\,218…≈-0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to 6 decimal places. \n\nSince $-0.927\\,295$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=-0.927\\,295…+\\pi ≈2.214\\,297 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=-0.927\\,295…+2\\pi ≈5.355\\,890\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈2.214\\,297\\cdot 5.355\\,890≈11.860\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354246 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354650 1069 "q-8845" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354650 1071 "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})+1)\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354650 1072 #uuid "b284307a-7bac-5101-a812-75d87d197409" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354650 1074 "With $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{vx}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x})+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v+1) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo evaluate the integral on the left-hand side, we use integration by substitution with\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{ln}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{v\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{u}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡v| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡v| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{C}|x|) \\\\ |\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x}| & =|x|{e}^{C} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x} & =\\pm {e}^{C}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{y}{x} & =Kx \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & ={e}^{Kx} \\\\ y & =x{e}^{Kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced the new constant of integration:\n\n$$\nK=\\pm {e}^{C}\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354010 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354651 1069 "q-88458" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354651 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2}=0?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354651 1072 #uuid "5ebf2795-4a5b-5fc4-bae2-4108180a784e" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354651 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-7x+12){3}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-7x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=3,4.$\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=3,4,$ and their product is $3\\cdot 4=12.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353858 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354652 1069 "q-88460" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354652 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3}=0?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354652 1072 #uuid "395668d0-2b32-5884-a30f-d67a196224fd" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354652 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-9x+4){6}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4,$ and their product is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4=2.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354211 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354653 1069 "q-88473" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354653 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}=7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354653 1072 #uuid "dc805550-3caa-5380-82bc-9ef272d45b26" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354653 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}+4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-7\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+4x-7)\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+7)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot 1=-\\frac{7}{3}.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353950 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354654 1069 "q-88474" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354654 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x}=6x\\cdot {5}^{x}?$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354654 1072 #uuid "23aad5f9-739e-5764-b74b-f1c4da81d658" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354654 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =6x\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-6x\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x-45)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)(x-5)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $3=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3)\\cdot 5=-15.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354117 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354655 1069 "q-88495" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354655 1071 "Solve the inequality $x(x+2)(x-2)(x-6)>0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354655 1072 #uuid "b7877d68-b9fc-5193-a68e-4210074e245a" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354655 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x(x+2)(x-2)(x-6).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2$ and $x=6.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,6),\\,(6,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(0,2)$ and $(6,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353962 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354656 1069 "q-88585" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354656 1071 "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354656 1072 #uuid "817bf530-201f-540c-9162-aa1369ea1581" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354656 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡((-x)^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354120 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354657 1069 "q-88586" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354657 1071 "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354657 1072 #uuid "9954fd5b-c505-5a49-9b52-d14339697167" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354657 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{4}\\,{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353873 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354658 1069 "q-88655" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354658 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{x-3}{x+1}<0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354658 1072 #uuid "4672614d-846a-573f-8b27-3adbd4fc1ee8" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354658 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(-1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,3).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353988 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354659 1069 "q-88656" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354659 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{x+2}{2x+1}\\le 0.$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354659 1072 #uuid "4e513f7c-913c-5acd-9b48-e4a489e60188" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354659 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=-2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$  The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354236 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354660 1069 "q-88689" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354660 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354660 1072 #uuid "ebd12802-ac6e-5b21-a42e-2abc0f87cbb7" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354660 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353947 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354661 1069 "q-88692" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354661 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354661 1072 #uuid "6956ed56-9d47-53cf-b35c-82d26f52b6fb" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354661 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353845 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354662 1069 "q-88695" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354662 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354662 1072 #uuid "857f38ae-b398-5bec-99e3-9572a98abf10" 13194139533431 17592186045585 true] [17592186354662 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$  is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=(x+1)^{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h